== 17. Of the Master of the Family or Goodman of the House

So much for the various and distinct combinations or couples belonging to the state of household government. From these arise two persons of a mixed or compound nature and condition, commonly called the master and the mistress of the house.

The #emph[master of the family, or goodman of the house,] is a person in whom rests the private and particular government of the whole household. He does not come to it by election, as happens in other states, but by the ordinance of God, settled even in the order of nature. The husband indeed naturally bears rule over the wife, parents over their children, masters over their servants. But the person who by the providence of God has the place of a husband, a father and a master in his house, also has by the light of nature the chief place and sovereignty in it. He is the #emph[paterfamilias,] the father and chief head of the family. To him therefore the true right and power over all household matters rightfully belongs. The duties of the master of the family are chiefly five.

I. To bear the chief part, and to be the principal agent, director and furtherer of the worship of God within his family. Josh 24:15: “As for me and my house, we will serve the #smallcaps[Lord].” And this he does partly by praying for and with his household, and partly by instructing them in the holy Scriptures and in the grounds of religion, so that they may grow in knowledge and reap benefit from the public ministry. This is how God commanded the men of Israel to walk in their families. Deut 6:7: “And you shall teach them diligently to your children and speak of them when you sit at home … when you lie down and when you get up.” Again, v. 20: “When your son asks, ‘What is the meaning of the decrees and statutes and ordinances that the #smallcaps[Lord] our God has commanded you#super[+]?’ then you are to tell him, ‘We were slaves of Pharaoh in Egypt, but the #smallcaps[Lord] brought us out of Egypt with a mighty hand.’” God gave this testimony of Abraham before the law was given. Gen 18:19: “For I know him, that he will command his children and his household after him to keep the way of the #smallcaps[Lord].” And this household of Abraham was very large. For the Holy Spirit says long before that he had “318 trained men#footnote[\[Ethchanicau\] suos initiatos [his initiated ones].] born in his household” (Gen 14:14). It was probably the course of Solomon, which he had learned from his father David and given as a rule to all masters of families. Prov 4:1: “Listen, my sons, to a father’s instruction; pay attention and gain understanding. … When I was a son to my father, tender and the only child of my mother, he taught me and said, ‘Let your heart lay hold of my words.’” The company of the disciples was the household of Christ, and he himself administered all parts of the service of God with them and among them (Luke 22:15). To this purpose#footnote[In Ioan. tract. 51. in fine.] Augustine says, “The master of the family in a way, and in his measure, performs the duty of a churchman, or bishop, within his house.”

#emph[II. To bring his family to the church or congregation on the Sabbath day; to see that they behave themselves there reverently; and when the public services are ended and the congregation is dismissed, to take an account of what they have heard, so that they may profit in knowledge and obedience.] Acts 10:24: “The following day he arrived in Caesarea, where Cornelius was expecting them and had called together his relatives and close friends.” And v. 33: “Now then,” says Cornelius to Peter, “we are all here in the presence of God to listen to everything the Lord has instructed you to tell us.” Job 1:5: “And when the days of feasting were over, Job would send for his children to purify them, rising early in the morning to offer burnt offerings,” etc. 2 Kgs 4:23: “‘Why would you go to him today?’ he replied. ‘It is not a New Moon or a Sabbath.’” #emph[For this very reason the fourth commandment is given first of all and chiefly to the master of the family, so that he might see the Sabbath kept and be a principal doer in all parts of God’s worship on it.] Exod 20:10: “On it you must not do any work—neither you, nor your son or daughter, nor your manservant or maidservant or livestock, nor the foreigner within your gates.”

Nor is this care of his tied only to the Sabbath. On other days besides, he ought to see that those of his household go, as far as lies in him, to places where the word of God is preached. Commendable in this case was the practice of Mary the sister of Martha, who, when Christ came to her house, is said to have “sat at the Lord’s feet listening to His message” (Luke 10:39).

III. To provide food, drink and clothing for his family, and that they may live a quiet and peaceable life (1 Tim 5:8). Prov 27:26: “The lambs will provide you with clothing, and the goats with the price of a field.” And v. 27: “You will have plenty of goats’ milk to feed you—food for your household and nourishment for your maidservants.” 1 Cor 9:9: “‘Do not muzzle an ox while it is treading out the grain.’ Is it about oxen that God is concerned? Isn’t He actually speaking on our behalf?” Matt 13:52: “Every scribe who has been discipled in the kingdom of heaven is like a householder who brings out of his storeroom new treasures as well as old.”

IV. To keep order and to exercise discipline in his house, and that in this manner.

#emph[In case of offense, when a capital crime is committed which incurs public censure, he is not to punish it himself, but to bring the offender to the civil magistrate and inform him of the offender’s fault, so that the offender may receive what he deserves. This course was established by the judicial law which God gave to Moses for his direction in criminal cases among the Israelites.] Deut 21:18, 19, 20: “If a man has a stubborn and rebellious son who does not obey his father and mother and does not listen to them when disciplined, his father and mother are to lay hold of him and bring him to the elders of his city, to the gate of his hometown, and say to the elders, ‘This son of ours is stubborn and rebellious; he does not obey us. He is a glutton and a drunkard.’” Again, Deut 13:6: “If your very own brother, or your son or daughter, or the wife you embrace, or your closest friend secretly entices you, saying, ‘Let us go and worship other gods’ (which neither you nor your fathers have known …), you must not yield to him or listen to him. Show him no pity, and do not spare him or shield him.” Zech 13:3: “And if anyone still prophesies, his father and mother who bore him will say to him, ‘You shall not remain alive, because you have spoken falsely in the name of the #smallcaps[Lord].’ When he prophesies, his father and mother who bore him will pierce him through.”

If the fault is of a lesser nature and smaller by comparison, the master of the family ought to proceed by private censure against the offending party: at times by admonition, at other times by correction and chastisement, according to the quality of the offense and the condition and state of the person. Lev 19:17: “Directly rebuke your neighbor, and do not let him go on in sin.” Matt 18:15: “If your brother sins against you, go and confront him privately. … But if he will not listen, take one or two others along.” Prov 29:17: “Discipline your son, and he will give you rest; he will bring delight to your soul.”

When admonitions and corrections will not prevail, the party must be brought before the ministers and governors of the Church, so that they may censure him. Matt 18:17: “If he refuses to listen to them, tell it to the church.” And the apostle James gives order that the ministers of the Church should come to those who are sick and pray for them (Jas 5:14). If they are to do this in a case of sickness, then they are also to be sought out for redress in a case of error and offense committed.

#emph[But when none of all these means will do good, if the party is a servant, his master may, and ought to, remove him.] Ps 101:7: “No one who practices deceit shall dwell in my house; no one who tells lies shall stand in my presence.” John 8:35: “A servant does not remain in the house forever.”

#emph[V. To give hospitality to those who are strangers and not of the family, if they are Christians and believers, but especially to the ministers of the Word.] Rom 12:13: “Practice hospitality.” Heb 13:2: “Do not neglect to show hospitality to strangers, for by so doing some people have entertained angels without knowing it.” #emph[The woman of Shunem and her husband performed this duty for the prophet] Elisha. 2 Kgs 4:10: “Please let us make a small room upstairs and put in it a bed, a table, a chair, and a lamp for him. Then when he comes to us, he can stay there.” #emph[These are the duties.]

Here it may be asked whether the master of a family is always and necessarily bound to do all these things in his own person.

I answer: if there is just and necessary cause, either because of his own inability or because of public employment in a lawful calling, by which he is hindered so that at some times he cannot do any of them himself, it is lawful for him to have and use a deputy. Our Savior Christ teaches this in effect when he says, “Who then is the faithful and wise servant, whom the master has put in charge of his household, to give the others their food at the proper time?” (Matt 24:45). So Abraham had a steward of his house, Eliezer of Damascus (Gen 15:2). And he carried out the weightiest matters that concerned the establishment and continuance of his family, not by himself, but by his steward, namely the choice of a wife for his son Isaac. Gen 24:2: “So Abraham instructed the chief servant of his household, who managed all he owned, ‘Place your hand under my thigh,’” etc. So Pharaoh king of Egypt had Joseph. Gen 39:4: “Joseph found favor in his sight and became his personal attendant. He put him in charge of his household and entrusted him with everything he owned.”
